      "Runs on DeWalt 20V MAX, the most widely stocked platform in US stores"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Small compact batteries; heavy deck work needs a bigger pack",
      "Battery capacity is listed as 1.3Ah on DeWalt's page and 1.5Ah by some retailers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best balance of price, brushless performance and platform for most homeowners and DIYers.",
    "description": "The DCK277C2 is the kit we would point most people to. It pairs DeWalt's DCD777 compact brushless 1/2-inch drill/driver with the DCF787 brushless 1/4-inch impact driver, two compact 20V MAX batteries, a DCB107 charger and a contractor bag. DeWalt rates the DCF787 at 1,500 in-lbs of maximum torque, 0-2,800 RPM and 0-3,200 impacts per minute, which covers decking screws and most lag work. Both tools are brushless, so they get more work out of the small packs than the brushed DCK240C2. The batteries are the compromise: DeWalt's own page lists them as 1.3Ah, while several retailers list 1.5Ah, and either way they are compact packs. Owners commonly add a 4.0Ah or 5.0Ah pack for bigger jobs. Since the kit gets you onto 20V MAX, every DeWalt bare tool, from saws to blowers, runs on those batteries."
  },
  {
